WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.090
- It's possible that Marvel
might lose the rights

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:04.380
to some of its most popular
characters: Spider-Man,

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.110
Black Widow, Thor, and
Iron Man, among others.

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:10.370
It's possible they might be
ripped right out of the MCU,

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:12.373
because copyright is weird.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:16.440
But Marvel is being proactive.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.700
That's why they're going
to court to stop the heirs

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:21.170
of the creators of some of
its most iconic characters

00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:22.750
from getting any royalties.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:24.240
With these lawsuits, Marvel is arguing

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.680
that characters created
by geniuses like Stan Lee

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:28.430
are ineligible for copyright termination

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:30.350
because they were works made for hire.

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:32.540
In other words, since Lee
was a Marvel employee,

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.470
his estate is not entitled
to any of the profits

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:36.680
from future TV, movies, and merchandise.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:38.870
And Marvel makes the same
argument against Steve Ditko,

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:41.590
Gene Colan, and Larry
Lieber, or their estates.

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:43.250
Ditko, of course, is the reclusive legend

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:44.840
who created Spider-Man with Stan Lee.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:46.470
Colan is the genius behind Daredevil,

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:48.970
Falcon, Carol Danvers as Mrs. Marvel,

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:51.070
and the cult satire Howard the Duck.

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:52.360
Lieber drew newspaper strips

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.090
of "The Incredible Hulk"
and "The Amazing Spider-Man"

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.130
and was instrumental in creating Iron Man.

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:57.410
Marvel is still making movies

00:00:57.410 --> 00:00:58.930
and TV shows featuring these characters,

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:01.490
and they are a big part of
Disney's long-term plan.

00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:03.910
Well, maybe not Howard the
Duck, at least not yet,

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:05.910
but if that dog killer Cruella de Vil

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:07.580
can get a favorable origin story,

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:08.750
we know that Howard the Duck

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:10.760
probably can't be too far behind.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:12.070
- That's it.

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:14.420
No more Mr. Nice Duck.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:15.710
- But this is no small matter.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:17.260
If Marvel loses these lawsuits,

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.830
its parent company Disney
would have to share ownership

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.771
of these characters that are
worth billions of dollars.

00:01:22.771 --> 00:01:26.140
(stately music)

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:27.280
Hey, Legal Eagles, it's time

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:29.090
to think like a copyright lawyer.

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:32.210
So let's talk about why is
Marvel suing the creators

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:35.140
of some of its most famous
characters and what's at stake.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:36.650
Let's take a look at what prompted Marvel

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:37.690
to file these lawsuits.

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:39.730
Steve Ditko was a
renowned comic book artist

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:40.730
who collaborated with Stan Lee

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:42.520
to create Spider-Man and Doctor Strange.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.510
And unlike Lee, Ditko was a recluse

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:46.120
who preferred to live in privacy,

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:47.550
much like author J. D. Salinger.

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.190
In his post-Marvel career,

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:51.570
Ditko invented cult
favorites like Squirrel Girl

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:53.700
and wrote several comics
featuring objectivist characters

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.959
modeled on the philosophy of Ayn Rand.

00:01:55.959 --> 00:01:58.530
Ditko lived and worked
alone in a Manhattan studio

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:01.270
until his death at the age of 90 in 2018.

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:04.050
And no one knows what Ditko
worked on in his last years,

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:06.200
but his last published words came in 2019.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.270
Quote: "Here's to those who wish me well,

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:10.470
and those that don't can go to hell."

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.000
And in this case, those who can go to hell

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.760
might include Marvel and Disney.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:16.900
- Oh, dear, what an awkward situation.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:18.990
- Now, this isn't always
the case, but sometimes,

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:20.350
there are termination provisions

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:22.710
under copyright law where
authors or their heirs

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:24.730
can reclaim rights once
granted to publishers

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:27.280
after waiting a statutorily
set period of time.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:28.220
Ditko's heirs, in this case,

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:30.000
include his brother and his nephew.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.760
In August, his estate filed a notice

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.400
of termination on Spider-Man.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:35.030
The Ditko termination notice demands

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:36.980
that Marvel must relinquish Ditko's rights

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:40.060
in Spider-Man by June of 2023,

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:41.930
and there are termination provisions

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:43.870
potentially at issue with a whole host

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:46.160
of some of Marvel's
most famous characters.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:50.000
So just what is a termination
provision in US copyright law?

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.000
Well, copyright termination
is the termination

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.640
of a grant or transfer of
someone's copyright rights,

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.320
and the Copyright Act of 1976

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:58.260
lengthened the term of
copyright protection,

00:02:58.260 --> 00:02:59.840
giving copyright owners even more time

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:02.480
to control and profit
from their works of art.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.420
This was the major policy accomplishment

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:06.380
of congressman Sonny Bono,

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:08.610
otherwise known as 1/2 of Sonny &amp; Cher.

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:12.970
Yes, he did go on from the duo
to hold office in Congress.

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:16.320
And many people have either
blamed or congratulated Disney

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.950
on this extension of the copyright term.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:21.010
But note that not all copyright holders

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:23.000
are the original creators of the art.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:24.830
For example, Paul McCartney co-wrote

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:26.780
some of the most memorable
songs ever written,

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:28.300
but over the years, he's sold or lost

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:29.710
the copyright to that material.

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:31.270
Most of the Beatles'
catalog is currently owned

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:34.040
by Sony/ATV, which
acquired a good chunk of it

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:35.730
from the estate of Michael Jackson,

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:38.510
who famously purchased
them for $47 million.

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:41.320
♪ Yeah, yeah, ooh, ooh, yeah ♪

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:43.810
In 2018, McCartney filed
termination notices

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:46.060
to reclaim those Beatles copyrights.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:47.950
Eventually, he settled with Sony/ATV.

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:49.690
A decision on the merits of that case

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:51.110
would have been a landmark ruling,

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:52.470
but both sides obviously felt

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:54.260
there was too much to risk to proceed.

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:56.170
And with the value of the
Beatles publishing rights

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:58.970
over $1 billion, there's
plenty of money to go around.

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:01.930
Now, creators can lose or
voluntarily assign rights

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:03.770
in a number of different
ways, including just entering

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:05.850
into a contract that
seemed fair at the time,

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:08.020
but in retrospect turned
out to be very one-sided.

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:10.930
Now, the amended Copyright Act
therefore gave some artists

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:12.750
the chance to get their copyrights back,

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:14.040
provided that they lost their rights

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:17.020
on or before January 1st, 1978.

00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:18.380
So the idea was that Congress

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:19.720
is extending the copyright term,

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:22.510
so if you had a copyright
and then transferred it

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:26.010
to somebody else before
1978, then you were allowed

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:28.230
to claw back that copyright assignment

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:30.990
or transfer many decades later,

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:34.190
and now a lot of those
terms are coming due.

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:35.400
Following termination, any rights

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:37.210
transferred under the grant come back

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:39.330
to the person who created
the copyrighted work.

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:41.630
The purpose of this provision
is to let authors, musicians,

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:43.770
and illustrators and other
artists renegotiate the terms

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:45.640
of their original contractual agreements.

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:47.380
It basically gives artists a second chance

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:48.400
to get a better deal.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:50.540
If the author is living,
like Larry Lieber,

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:53.107
they can file a termination
notice at the appropriate time.

00:04:53.107 --> 00:04:54.760
Lieber is Stan Lee's little brother.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:56.100
He's responsible for co-creating

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.810
Ant-Man, Thor, and Iron Man.

00:04:57.810 --> 00:04:59.290
Marvel is suing Lieber to stop him

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:00.730
from terminating the Marvel copyrights

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:02.520
to these characters, or in other words,

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:05.500
Marvel is going to court to
say that in this situation,

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:07.240
the termination provisions don't apply

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.870
and the rights therefore don't
have to go anywhere else.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:12.990
But not all rights return
after a successful termination.

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:15.840
Creators' American rights
revert, but foreign rights,

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:17.190
which are usually negotiated separately,

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:18.670
do not return to the creator.

00:05:18.670 --> 00:05:20.880
Derivative works created under
the authority of the grant

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:23.340
before its termination can
continue to be exploited,

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:25.870
and as you can see, this
is a more complex situation

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:26.760
than it first appears.

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:29.450
So let's dive into the Copyright
Act provisions at stake:

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:31.770
17 U.S.C. 203 and 304.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:32.820
The first question that arises

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:34.850
is which works are
eligible for termination.

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:37.480
Although most grants of copyright
rights can be terminated,

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:38.770
some grants are excluded.

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:40.230
Since the termination provisions are meant

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:41.950
to give the author a second chance,

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:43.000
if the author is still living

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:45.430
and the original grants
were made by somebody else,

00:05:45.430 --> 00:05:46.760
these grants can't be terminated.

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:48.440
If the author is living, grants made

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:50.530
by anyone other than
certain statutory heirs

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:52.500
are also ineligible for termination.

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:55.520
The list of individuals is
included in section 304.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:57.800
And the act also excludes
grants made by will,

00:05:57.800 --> 00:05:59.720
so if an author died 50 years ago

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:02.020
and granted their copyrights
to someone by will,

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:03.530
that person's heirs can't attempt

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:05.310
to revoke the grant under this section.

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:07.370
Additionally, and this is the big one,

00:06:07.370 --> 00:06:08.930
the Copyright Act's termination provisions

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:11.170
do not apply to works made for hire.

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:13.450
A work made for hire has
a complicated definition,

00:06:13.450 --> 00:06:15.810
but generally speaking, if you're employed

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:18.350
when you make something
that could be copyrighted,

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:21.410
it's your employer who gets
the copyright to that thing.

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:23.760
It's not the employee who made that thing

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:25.550
while being employed by somebody else.

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:27.310
And if a work is made for hire,

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:28.640
an employer is considered the author,

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.930
even if the employee
actually created the work.

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:32.130
The employer can be a firm,

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:33.830
an organization, or an individual.

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:36.200
This is a very contentious part of the law

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:37.190
for comic book creators,

00:06:37.190 --> 00:06:38.720
many of whom were actually employees

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:40.210
when they created their characters.

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:42.110
And determining whether
something is a work for hire

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:43.660
is challenging and depends on the facts

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:45.080
of each particular situation.

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:46.640
Now, there are some complications

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:48.740
in when the termination
notice needs to be provided.

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:49.960
I won't bore you with the details,

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:51.240
but it generally has to fall

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:52.950
within a five-year termination period,

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:55.130
and here, the Marvel cases
mostly involve grants

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:57.300
executed before January 1st, 1978.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:58.580
And in addition to the timing,

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:01.350
there are tons of procedural
issues, like co-ownership.

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:03.330
A majority of the co-owners have to agree.

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:05.790
There are lots and lots
of procedural issues here

00:07:05.790 --> 00:07:07.760
that are extremely
boring and have no place

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:09.140
in a 15-minute YouTube video.

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:11.330
And other creators have filed
termination notices as well.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:13.940
They tend to be represented
by attorney Marc Toberoff,

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:16.240
the lawyer who represented
"Superman" creators Jerry Siegel

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:18.800
and Joe Schuster in their
ultimately unsuccessful

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:20.940
termination attempt against DC Comics.

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:22.260
Now, under some circumstances,

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:24.040
a would-be defendant can go into court

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:25.520
and get the court to adjudicate the issue

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:27.250
before everything goes crazy,

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:28.880
and that's what Marvel has done here.

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:31.070
Marvel has filed suit
against the creators,

00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:32.930
alleging that they were all employees

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:34.120
and that the works that they created

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:35.440
were considered works for hire

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.600
and thus not subject to
copyright termination.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:39.410
Marvel is asking federal
courts in New York

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:41.850
and California to issue
declaratory judgments

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:43.550
blocking the termination
notices and affirming

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:45.900
that Disney is the sole
owner of these characters.

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:48.860
So you might see headlines that
Disney is suing the owners,

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:49.820
and it sounds nefarious,

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:51.210
but there's really nothing wrong with it.

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:53.600
They're adjudicating this case early,

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:56.080
and while Disney would
normally be the defendant

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:57.960
in an action like this,

00:07:57.960 --> 00:07:59.450
they're really just going to the court

00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:01.630
to adjudicate exactly the
same things that they would

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:05.020
if the creators were the
ones that were suing Disney.

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:06.730
Now, a lot of this
litigation is going to turn

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:08.980
on the definition of a work made for hire.

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:11.130
That's defined by section
101 of the Copyright Act,

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:13.070
which defines a work made
for hire in two parts.

00:08:13.070 --> 00:08:16.080
It can be either A, a work
prepared by an employee

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:18.400
within the scope of his
or her employment, or B,

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:20.980
a work specially ordered
or commissioned for use

00:08:20.980 --> 00:08:22.660
as a contribution to a collective work,

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:25.130
as part of a motion picture
or other audiovisual work,

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:26.890
as a translation, as a supplementary work,

00:08:26.890 --> 00:08:29.090
as a compilation, as
an instructional text,

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:31.760
as a test, as an answer
material for a test,

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:33.850
or as an atlas if the
parties expressly agree

00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:35.500
in a written instrument signed by them

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:37.617
that the work shall be
considered a work made for hire.

00:08:37.617 --> 00:08:39.780
And the Supreme Court has
interpreted a work for hire

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:42.090
in the famous case of CCNV vs. Reid.

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:44.470
Now, in that case, the Community
for Creative Non-Violence

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:46.770
made an oral agreement with
James Reid, a sculptor,

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:49.550
to produce a statue depicting
the plight of the homeless

00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:52.640
for display in a 1985
Washington DC Christmas pageant.

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:54.550
After the sculpture was
completed and delivered,

00:08:54.550 --> 00:08:56.380
CCNV paid Reid in full.

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:58.410
Shortly after that, both
parties filed separate

00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:00.580
and competing copyright
claims over the sculpture.

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:01.420
A lower court held

00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:03.290
that the sculpture was
a work made for hire,

00:09:03.290 --> 00:09:05.020
and after the Second Circuit
reversed the decision,

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:06.410
it went all the way to the Supreme Court.

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:08.390
And the Supreme Court ruled
for Reid, the sculptor,

00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:09.970
holding that the court
must first ascertain

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:11.870
whether the work was
prepared by an employee

00:09:11.870 --> 00:09:13.200
or an independent contractor.

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:14.480
If an employee created the work,

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:16.670
part one of the definition
that we talked about applies,

00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:17.850
and the work will generally be considered

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:19.290
a work made for hire because it was made

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:21.070
within the scope of that
person's employment.

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:22.380
But this gets more complicated,

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:24.200
because the term employee
in the definition

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:26.390
differs from the common
understanding of the term.

00:09:26.390 --> 00:09:28.440
For purposes of the
Copyright Act, an employee

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:31.920
means an employee under the
general common law of agency.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:33.970
If an independent
contractor created the work,

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:36.050
then the work was specially
ordered or commissioned.

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:37.730
Part two of the definition applies.

00:09:37.730 --> 00:09:39.140
And an independent contractor is someone

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:40.190
who was not an employee

00:09:40.190 --> 00:09:42.000
under the general common law of agency.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:43.550
And the Supreme Court created four factors

00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:45.760
to determine whether
someone is an employee

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:47.580
under purposes of the Copyright Act.

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:49.030
I won't bore you with those definitions,

00:09:49.030 --> 00:09:51.260
but you can actually
read it in CCNV vs. Reid.

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:54.090
It's actually a pretty
understandable court decision,

00:09:54.090 --> 00:09:55.260
as those things go.

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:57.580
But in this litigation,
it will probably focus

00:09:57.580 --> 00:10:00.340
on what's called the
Marvel Method, which is a,

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:02.380
quote, "loose collaborative
working atmosphere

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:04.230
where initial ideas were briefly discussed

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:07.270
with artists responsible for
taking care of the details."

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:08.500
Marvel characterizes this

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:11.330
as a traditional
employer-employee relationship,

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:13.040
and Marvel argues that any contributions

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:14.870
that Steve Ditko, for example, made

00:10:14.870 --> 00:10:17.340
were at Marvel's instance and expense.

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:19.020
Marvel editorial staff have the right

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:20.170
to exercise creative control

00:10:20.170 --> 00:10:21.890
over Steve Ditko's contributions,

00:10:21.890 --> 00:10:24.050
and Marvel paid Steve
Ditko a per-page rate

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:25.530
for his contributions.

00:10:25.530 --> 00:10:26.960
When Steve Ditko worked for Marvel,

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:29.460
he did so with the expectation
that Marvel would pay him.

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:31.970
Moreover, Marvel says that its
editorial staff had the right

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:33.160
to exercise creative control

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:34.630
over Steve Ditko's contributions.

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:36.700
Quote: "Any contributions Steve Ditko made

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:38.330
to the Works were done at Marvel's expense

00:10:38.330 --> 00:10:39.820
because Marvel paid Steve Ditko

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:42.090
a per-page rate for his contributions,

00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:44.290
Steve Ditko made those
contributions to the Works

00:10:44.290 --> 00:10:46.210
with the expectation that
Marvel would pay him,

00:10:46.210 --> 00:10:48.550
and Steve Ditko did not
obtain any ownership interest

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:50.570
in or to his contributions."

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:52.220
And Marvel made the same arguments

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:53.740
in the other lawsuits as well,

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:55.560
and the representatives
of the comic book creators

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:57.750
will make the same legal
arguments that Jack Kirby did

00:10:57.750 --> 00:11:00.470
over a decade ago when Kirby
and Marvel litigated the issue

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:03.120
of whether Kirby could
terminate a copyright grant

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:05.240
on "Spider-Man," "X-Men,"
"The Incredible Hulk,"

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:06.450
and "Mighty Thor."

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:08.250
In 2013, the Second
Circuit Court of Appeals

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:09.530
affirmed a lower court ruling

00:11:09.530 --> 00:11:11.270
that Kirby's heirs could not terminate

00:11:11.270 --> 00:11:12.670
the copyright over those characters,

00:11:12.670 --> 00:11:15.010
since Kirby had contributed his materials

00:11:15.010 --> 00:11:16.380
as a work made for hire.

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:18.190
Kirby tried to appeal
to the Supreme Court,

00:11:18.190 --> 00:11:20.160
arguing that he was
essentially a freelancer,

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:21.190
not an employee.

00:11:21.190 --> 00:11:23.590
Kirby didn't receive any
benefits or fixed salary,

00:11:23.590 --> 00:11:25.780
nor did Marvel reimburse
him for his expenses.

00:11:25.780 --> 00:11:28.220
Kirby also worked from home,
and although this would seem

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:30.270
to suggest that he was an
independent contractor,

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:32.150
the Second Circuit saw
the situation differently.

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:33.280
Quote: "Despite the absence

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:34.860
of a formal employment agreement,

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:36.310
however, the record suggests

00:11:36.310 --> 00:11:38.350
that Kirby and Marvel
were closely affiliated

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:39.780
during the relevant time period.

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:42.330
Lee assigned Kirby, whom he
considered his best artist,

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:44.210
a steady stream of work
during that period,

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:46.230
and Kirby seems to have
done most of his work

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:47.630
with Marvel projects in mind."

00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:48.620
The Second Circuit emphasized

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:50.290
that the key factor is
whether a work is made

00:11:50.290 --> 00:11:52.550
at the hiring party's
instance and expense,

00:11:52.550 --> 00:11:54.120
and that happens when the employer

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:55.480
induces the creation of the work

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:57.210
and has the right to direct and supervise

00:11:57.210 --> 00:11:59.010
the manner in which the
work is carried out.

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:00.740
The Second Circuit
acknowledged that Kirby's,

00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:02.200
quote, "working relationship with Marvel

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:04.660
between the years of 1958 and 1963

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:07.157
was close and continuous,"
and continued, quote,

00:12:07.157 --> 00:12:09.560
"Understood as products of
the overarching relationship,

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:11.390
Kirby's works during this period

00:12:11.390 --> 00:12:13.040
were hardly self-directed projects

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:14.040
in which he hoped Marvel,

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:15.770
as one of several potential publishers,

00:12:15.770 --> 00:12:16.603
might have an interest.

00:12:16.603 --> 00:12:18.160
Rather, he created the relevant works

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:19.500
pursuant to Marvel's assignment

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:21.700
or with Marvel specifically in mind.

00:12:21.700 --> 00:12:23.190
Kirby's ongoing partnership with Marvel,

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:26.120
however unbalanced and
under-remunerative to the artist,

00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:29.540
is therefore what induced
Kirby's creation of the works."

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:31.660
It's believed that Kirby
had a looser agreement

00:12:31.660 --> 00:12:34.110
with Marvel than many other
artists, including Lee.

00:12:34.110 --> 00:12:36.360
And when the Supreme Court
declined to accept the appeal

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:38.420
from the Kirby case, the parties settled.

00:12:38.420 --> 00:12:39.500
And since the ruling only applied

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:41.100
to two of Kirby's four children,

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:43.600
the others lived in California,
Marvel opted to settle

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:45.640
with the family rather
than face more litigation.

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:47.700
So this probably means
that Marvel has the edge

00:12:47.700 --> 00:12:49.830
because of this published
Second Circuit ruling.

00:12:49.830 --> 00:12:50.663
In the current cases,

00:12:50.663 --> 00:12:53.040
Marvel argues that the
Kirby case is dispositive

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:55.140
and that the courts
must rule in its favor.

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:57.230
However, the ultimate issue
of whether the Marvel Method

00:12:57.230 --> 00:13:00.090
of producing comics means that
the creators were employees

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:02.700
still hasn't been decided
by the Supreme Court.

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:04.270
And one of the most important factors

00:13:04.270 --> 00:13:05.970
in determining whether you are an employee

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:07.000
or an independent contractor

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:08.500
